// FPMS-OS: nothing on this board updates itself on a timer.
//
// Installed by the overlay copy in scripts/50-overlay.sh; verified by
// scripts/70-update-policy.sh with `apt-config dump`, which is also the
// syntax check - a malformed file here breaks EVERY apt operation on the
// image, not just this policy.
//
// FILENAME: no extension, like its neighbours 20auto-upgrades and
// 50unattended-upgrades. apt reads a file in apt.conf.d only if it ends in
// `.conf` or has no extension at all. The `70` prefix is load-bearing:
// apt.conf.d is read in alphanumeric order and the LAST assignment to a
// scalar wins, so 70 overrides whatever 20auto-upgrades says. Do not renumber
// it below 50.
//
// ===========================================================================
// THE DECISION: automatic apt activity is OFF. Security updates are a
// deliberate, foreground, operator-initiated act.
// ===========================================================================
//
// This is a competition robot, and the argument is not "updates are bad" - it
// is that on THIS board every property that makes unattended updating safe on
// a server is absent:
//
//  1. POWER IS A SWITCH. The rover is battery-powered and gets switched off
//     abruptly, by a human, at the end of a run. dpkg unpacking a package at
//     that moment leaves a half-configured package and /var/lib/dpkg/updates
//     behind, and the next apt refuses outright with "dpkg was interrupted".
//     scripts/00-base-system.sh has a whole repair block for exactly this
//     state because it has happened repeatedly ON THE BUILD HOST, where
//     someone was watching. On the rover nobody is watching, and the symptom
//     surfaces as a service that will not reinstall on race morning.
//
//  2. THE LINK IS ALREADY SATURATED. FPMS_CAMERA_FPS=6 and
//     FPMS_JPEG_QUALITY=40 are shipped deliberately low because higher
//     settings saturated WiFi and STARVED THE LIDAR FEED (SPEC.md section 5).
//     A background apt download is tens to hundreds of megabytes over that
//     same hotspot. The failure it produces is not "the update was slow", it
//     is a rover whose obstacle guard goes stale mid-mission.
//
//  3. RESTARTS ARE NOT CHEAP HERE. A package upgrade restarts services. The
//     micro-ROS serial link costs 90-225 s to re-establish (SPEC.md section
//     3) and takes the rover's only pose source with it, and a restart of
//     fpms-cored is a window with no STOP authority. Neither is acceptable at
//     a time chosen by a timer.
//
//  4. NO CONSOLE. There is no monitor on this board. Anything that goes wrong
//     unattended is invisible until someone notices the rover is unreachable,
//     which is the same symptom as a flat battery, a wrong SSID, and a dead
//     WiFi driver.
//
//  5. THE VENUE IS WHEN IT WOULD FIRE. The rover normally lives on an
//     operator laptop's hotspot at 192.168.137.1 with no route to the
//     internet. The days it DOES have internet are the days someone brought
//     it somewhere - i.e. exactly the days you least want a 3 a.m. upgrade.
//
// AND THE OTHER SIDE, HONESTLY: never patching is a real cost. mosquitto,
// rosbridge, fpms_console and sshd all bind 0.0.0.0 (SPEC.md section 7), and
// on a shared venue network that is genuine exposure. So this policy does NOT
// remove the ability to take security updates - it removes the TIMER. The
// unattended-upgrades security-only configuration is still written, still
// live, and still verifiable (see 71fpms-unattended-upgrades); an operator
// gets exactly that set, in the foreground, with:
//
//     sudo unattended-upgrade --dry-run -v     // what it WOULD do
//     sudo unattended-upgrade -v               // do it
//
// The human, not the clock, chooses the moment. docs/UPDATING.md says when.
//
// Three of the keys below are the ones that actually carry this
// (Update-Package-Lists / Download-Upgradeable-Packages / Unattended-Upgrade:
// /usr/lib/apt/apt.systemd.daily reads them and does nothing when they are
// 0). APT::Periodic::Enable is the belt-and-braces master switch; stage 70
// checks whether this image's apt.systemd.daily actually honours that name
// and says so in the build log rather than assuming it.
//
// NOTE ON WHAT IS *NOT* DONE HERE: apt-daily.timer and
// apt-daily-upgrade.timer are left alone. They are systemd units and this
// image's systemd units have one owner. The timers still fire; apt.systemd.daily
// reads this file and exits without touching the network or dpkg. If you want
// to confirm that on a rover: `systemctl list-timers apt-daily*` shows them
// scheduled, and `journalctl -u apt-daily-upgrade` shows them doing nothing.

APT::Periodic::Enable "0";
APT::Periodic::Update-Package-Lists "0";
APT::Periodic::Download-Upgradeable-Packages "0";
APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade "0";
APT::Periodic::AutocleanInterval "0";
APT::Periodic::CleanInterval "0";

// The rover's uplink is a hotspot that has been measured flapping on a 47 s
// cycle when WiFi power save comes back. One lost packet should not fail an
// operator's deliberate update.
Acquire::Retries "3";
